    'NASA',
    'Public domain',
    null,
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Sounds_of_Earth_-_GPN-2000-001976.jpg',
    array['How would an alien know how to play the record?', 'What music was chosen for the record?', 'Where are the Voyager spacecraft now?', 'How does the pulsar map show Earth''s location?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Voyager Golden Record',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_Golden_Record',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'placebo-effect',
    'The puzzling power of placebos',
    'A placebo is a treatment with no active ingredient, yet people taking one sometimes report real improvements, especially for symptoms such as pain and nausea. Expectation, conditioning and the natural ups and downs of illness all contribute; some apparent placebo effects are simply recovery that would have happened anyway. Placebos are central to clinical trials, where comparison against them helps reveal whether a treatment truly works.',
    'psychology',
    array['medicine', 'brain', 'expectations', 'research-methods']::text[],
    'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/Cebocap.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled',
    'Tablets of the kind used as placebos in trials',
    'NIH, objects donated by Elaine and Arthur Shapiro',
    'Public domain',
    null,
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cebocap.jpg',
    array['Can placebos work even when people know they are placebos?', 'What is the nocebo effect?', 'Why are double-blind trials important?', 'Which symptoms respond most to placebos?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Placebo',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Placebo',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'mirror-test',
    'Can animals recognise themselves?',
    'In the mirror test, devised by psychologist Gordon Gallup Jr. in 1970, an animal is marked somewhere it can only see in a reflection. If it investigates the mark on its own body, that suggests it recognises the reflection as itself. Chimpanzees, orangutans, some dolphins, an Asian elephant and Eurasian magpies have passed versions of the test. Critics note it favours vision-oriented species, so failing does not prove a lack of self-awareness.',
    'psychology',
    array['animals', 'intelligence', 'cognition', 'self-awareness']::text[],
    'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/43/Mirror_test_with_a_Baboon.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled',
    'A baboon looking at its reflection in a mirror',
    'Moshe Blank',
    'CC BY-SA 3.0',
    'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0',
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirror_test_with_a_Baboon.JPG',
    array['Why might dogs fail the mirror test?', 'At what age do human children pass the test?', 'Did cleaner fish really pass the mirror test?', 'Does passing mean an animal is self-aware?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Mirror test',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirror_test',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'mycorrhizal-networks',
    'The underground fungal web',
    'Most land plants partner with mycorrhizal fungi, whose fine threads extend far beyond the roots and trade water and minerals for sugars. Where fungal threads link several plants, they form common mycorrhizal networks. Popular accounts describe a ''wood wide web'' through which trees share food and warnings. Researchers agree the networks exist, but how much moves between trees through them, and whether it benefits forests broadly, is actively debated.',
    'nature',
    array['fungi', 'plants', 'forests', 'ecosystems']::text[],
    'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Amanita_muscaria_fruit_bodies.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled',
    'Fly agaric mushrooms, the fruiting bodies of a fungus that forms mycorrhizal partnerships with trees',
    'Wikimedia Commons contributor',
    'CC BY 2.5',
    'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.5',
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amanita_muscaria_fruit_bodies.jpg',
    array['What do plants and fungi gain from each other?', 'Why are claims about tree communication debated?', 'How old is the partnership between plants and fungi?', 'How do scientists trace nutrients between trees?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Mycorrhizal network',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mycorrhizal_network',
    3,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'bird-migration',
    'How birds navigate across continents',
    'Billions of birds migrate every year, some crossing oceans and deserts. Bar-tailed godwits have been tracked flying more than 11,000 kilometres non-stop from Alaska to New Zealand. Birds navigate using several cues: the Sun''s position, star patterns, landmarks, smells and Earth''s magnetic field. How birds sense magnetism is still being worked out, with leading hypotheses involving light-sensitive proteins in the eye.',
    'nature',
    array['birds', 'animals', 'navigation', 'magnetism']::text[],
    'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Bar-tailed_Godwit.jpg/1280px-Bar-tailed_Godwit.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
    'A bar-tailed godwit, a long-billed shorebird famous for non-stop migration',
    'Andreas Trepte',
    'CC BY-SA 2.5',
    'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5',
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bar-tailed_Godwit.jpg',
    array['How might birds sense Earth''s magnetic field?', 'How do birds fly for days without stopping?', 'How do scientists track small migrating birds?', 'Why do some birds migrate while others stay put?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Bird migration',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bird_migration',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'green-sahara',
    'When the Sahara was green',
    'Between roughly 11,000 and 5,000 years ago, much of the Sahara held grasslands, lakes and wetlands, a period called the African Humid Period. Slow, cyclical changes in Earth''s orbit strengthened the West African monsoon, carrying rain far north. Rock art showing cattle, giraffes and swimmers records the people who lived there. The return to desert happened at different speeds in different places, a timing scientists still investigate.',
    'geography',
    array['climate', 'deserts', 'africa', 'earth-history', 'archaeology']::text[],
    'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Sahara_satellite_hires.jpg/1280px-Sahara_satellite_hires.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
    'Satellite image of the Sahara Desert',
    'NASA',
    'Public domain',
    null,
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sahara_satellite_hires.jpg',
    array['How do changes in Earth''s orbit affect rainfall?', 'What does Saharan rock art reveal?', 'How quickly did the Sahara dry out?', 'Could the Sahara become green again?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: African humid period',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_humid_period',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'mariana-trench',
    'The deepest place on Earth',
    'The Challenger Deep, at the southern end of the Mariana Trench in the western Pacific, reaches nearly 11,000 metres below sea level, deeper than Mount Everest is tall. The trench forms where the Pacific Plate sinks beneath the Mariana Plate. Pressure at the bottom is over a thousand times that at the surface. Even so, crewed dives, first made in 1960, and robotic expeditions have found life there, including amphipods and microbes.',
    'geography',
    array['ocean', 'deep-sea', 'plate-tectonics', 'exploration']::text[],
    'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Marianatrenchmap.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled',
    'Map showing the location of the Mariana Trench in the western Pacific',
    'wallace',
    'CC BY 2.5',
    'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.5',
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marianatrenchmap.png',
    array['How did the first crewed dive reach the bottom?', 'What animals live in the deepest trenches?', 'How is the depth of the trench measured?', 'How do deep-sea creatures cope with the pressure?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Mariana Trench',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mariana_Trench',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'library-of-alexandria',
    'The real story of the Library of Alexandria',
    'Founded under the early Ptolemies, probably in the third century BCE, the Library of Alexandria became a leading centre of scholarship in the ancient world, supporting work in mathematics, astronomy, geography and literature. Popular stories describe a single catastrophic fire, but historians think the library declined gradually over centuries through reduced funding, political turmoil and several destructive events. How many scrolls it held is unknown; ancient figures vary enormously.',
    'history',
    array['ancient-world', 'books', 'egypt', 'scholarship']::text[],
    'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/64/Ancientlibraryalex.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled',
    'Nineteenth-century artistic depiction of the ancient Library of Alexandria',
    'O. Von Corven',
    'Public domain',
    null,
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancientlibraryalex.jpg',
    array['Did Julius Caesar really burn the library?', 'Which famous scholars worked at Alexandria?', 'How were books collected for the library?', 'What knowledge might have been lost?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Library of Alexandria',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Library_of_Alexandria',
    3,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'fibonacci-in-nature',
    'Fibonacci numbers in sunflowers and pine cones',
    'The Fibonacci sequence runs 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, each number the sum of the two before it. The numbers of spirals on sunflower heads and pine cones are often consecutive Fibonacci numbers. One explanation is that new seeds or scales grow at a fixed angle of about 137.5 degrees, the ''golden angle'', which packs them efficiently. Claims that the golden ratio appears everywhere, however, are frequently exaggerated.',
    'mathematics',
    array['numbers', 'patterns', 'plants', 'golden-ratio']::text[],
    'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Fibonacci_Squares.svg/1280px-Fibonacci_Squares.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
    'A spiral built from squares whose sides follow the Fibonacci sequence',
    'Romain',
    'CC BY-SA 4.0',
    'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0',
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fibonacci_Squares.svg',
    array['Why does the golden angle pack seeds efficiently?', 'How is the Fibonacci sequence related to the golden ratio?', 'Which golden ratio claims are myths?', 'Who was Fibonacci?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Fibonacci sequence',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fibonacci_sequence',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'invention-of-zero',
    'The surprisingly late invention of zero',
    'Babylonian scribes used a placeholder symbol to mark empty positions in numbers, and the Maya developed a zero independently. Treating zero as a number in its own right, with rules for arithmetic, was formalised in India; Brahmagupta described such rules in 628 CE. Through scholars writing in Arabic, the Indian numeral system reached Europe, where Fibonacci promoted it in 1202. Zero made place-value notation, and much of modern mathematics, possible.',
    'mathematics',
    array['numbers', 'history-of-science', 'india', 'ancient-world']::text[],
    null,
    null,
    null,
    null,
    null,
    null,
    array['Why did the Romans manage without zero?', 'What rules for zero did Brahmagupta propose?', 'How did Indian numerals reach Europe?', 'Why is dividing by zero undefined?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: 0',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/0',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'monty-hall-problem',
    'The game-show puzzle that fooled mathematicians',
    'You pick one of three doors; one hides a car, two hide goats. The host, who knows where the car is, opens another door to reveal a goat and offers you a switch. Switching wins two-thirds of the time, because your first pick was right only one-third of the time. When Marilyn vos Savant explained this in a 1990 magazine column, many readers, including people with doctorates, insisted she was wrong.',
    'mathematics',
    array['probability', 'puzzles', 'statistics', 'decision-making']::text[],
    'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Monty_open_door.svg/1280px-Monty_open_door.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
    'Diagram of three doors, one open to reveal a goat, illustrating the Monty Hall problem',
    'Cepheus',
    'Public domain',
    null,
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monty_open_door.svg',
    array['Why doesn''t the chance become 50:50 after a door opens?', 'What if the host opened a door at random?', 'How could I simulate the Monty Hall problem?', 'Why do our intuitions about probability fail?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Monty Hall problem',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monty_Hall_problem',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'the-transistor',
    'The switch that built the digital world',
    'In 1947, John Bardeen and Walter Brattain, working in William Shockley''s group at Bell Labs, demonstrated the first working transistor. Transistors use semiconductors to amplify signals or act as tiny switches, replacing bulky, power-hungry vacuum tubes. Packing them onto integrated circuits transformed computing. Modern processors contain billions of transistors, each far smaller than a bacterium, though further miniaturisation is increasingly difficult and expensive.',
    'technology',
    array['electronics', 'computing', 'inventions', 'semiconductors']::text[],
    'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e1/Transbauformen.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled',
    'Assorted discrete transistors in different packages',
    'Ulfbastel',
    'CC BY-SA 3.0',
    'http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/',
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Transbauformen.jpg',
    array['How does a transistor act as a switch?', 'What is Moore''s law, and is it ending?', 'Why are semiconductors like silicon so useful?', 'How are chips manufactured at such tiny scales?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Transistor',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transistor',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'gps-and-relativity',
    'Your phone''s map depends on Einstein',
    'GPS receivers calculate position by timing signals from several satellites, so the satellites'' atomic clocks must be extraordinarily precise. Relativity matters: because the satellites move fast, special relativity makes their clocks run slightly slow, while weaker gravity at their altitude makes them run fast, as general relativity predicts. The net effect is about 38 microseconds per day. Left uncorrected, position errors would quickly grow to kilometres.',
    'technology',
    array['satellites', 'relativity', 'physics', 'navigation']::text[],
    'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/GPS_Block_IIIA.jpg/1280px-GPS_Block_IIIA.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
    'Illustration of a GPS Block IIIA satellite',
    'U.S. Air Force',
    'Public domain',
    null,
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GPS_Block_IIIA.jpg',
    array['How does a receiver use timing to find its position?', 'Why does weaker gravity make clocks tick faster?', 'How accurate are atomic clocks?', 'How do systems like Galileo compare with GPS?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Global Positioning System',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Positioning_System',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'flying-buttresses',
    'How flying buttresses filled cathedrals with light',
    'Tall Gothic stone vaults push outward on their walls. Flying buttresses are arched supports that carry part of that thrust through open air to massive piers outside the building. By moving support outward, medieval builders could make walls thinner and fill them with large stained-glass windows, as at Chartres and Notre-Dame de Paris. They worked largely from experience and geometric rules, long before modern structural engineering existed.',
    'engineering',
    array['architecture', 'medieval', 'structures', 'gothic']::text[],
    'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/L%C3%BCbeck_Marienkirche_Strebeb%C3%B6gen.jpg/1280px-L%C3%BCbeck_Marienkirche_Strebeb%C3%B6gen.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
    'Flying buttresses on St. Mary''s Church in Lübeck',
    'Photo: Andreas Praefcke',
    'CC BY 3.0',
    'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0',
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%BCbeck_Marienkirche_Strebeb%C3%B6gen.jpg',
    array['How did medieval masons design without modern engineering?', 'Why are pointed arches useful in tall buildings?', 'How was medieval stained glass made?', 'How was Notre-Dame stabilised after the 2019 fire?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Flying buttress',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flying_buttress',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'panama-canal-locks',
    'Lifting ships over a continent',
    'The Panama Canal, opened in 1914, connects the Atlantic and Pacific without the long voyage around South America. Rather than cutting down to sea level, engineers built locks that raise ships about 26 metres to Gatun Lake and lower them on the other side. Each transit uses large volumes of fresh water from the lake system. Expanded locks, opened in 2016, take much larger ships and include basins that reuse part of the water.',
    'engineering',
    array['canals', 'infrastructure', 'ships', 'water']::text[],
    'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Panama_Canal_Map_EN.png/1280px-Panama_Canal_Map_EN.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
    'Map of the Panama Canal showing its locks and Gatun Lake',
    'Thoroe',
    'CC BY-SA 2.0',
    'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0',
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panama_Canal_Map_EN.png',
    array['How do canal locks raise a ship?', 'Why was the French attempt to build the canal abandoned?', 'How do droughts affect the canal?', 'How was disease controlled during construction?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Panama Canal',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panama_Canal',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'terracotta-army',
    'An army made for the afterlife',
    'In 1974, farmers digging a well near Xi''an, China, uncovered fragments of what proved to be thousands of life-sized clay soldiers. They were buried near the tomb of Qin Shi Huang, the first emperor of a unified China, who died in 210 BCE. Faces and details vary, and the figures were originally brightly painted. Much of the site, including the emperor''s burial chamber itself, has not been excavated.',
    'history',
    array['china', 'archaeology', 'ancient-world', 'art']::text[],
    'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/51714-Terracota-Army.jpg/1280px-51714-Terracota-Army.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
    'Rows of terracotta warrior statues in an excavation pit',
    'xiquinhosilva',
    'CC BY 2.0',
    'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0',
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:51714-Terracota-Army.jpg',
    array['How were thousands of figures made?', 'Why hasn''t the emperor''s tomb been opened?', 'What happened to the original paint?', 'Who was Qin Shi Huang?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Terracotta Army',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terracotta_Army',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'tyrian-purple',
    'The ancient dye reserved for the powerful',
    'Tyrian purple was produced in the ancient Mediterranean from secretions of predatory sea snails. Thousands of snails were needed for small amounts of dye, and the process was notoriously smelly. Because it was costly and colourfast, the colour became associated with power and status, and Roman laws at times restricted who could wear it. The dye takes its name from Tyre, a Phoenician city in what is now Lebanon.',
    'culture',
    array['colour', 'ancient-world', 'chemistry', 'fashion']::text[],
    'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Purple_Purpur_%28retouched%29.jpg/1280px-Purple_Purpur_%28retouched%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
    'A sample of deep Tyrian purple colour',
    'Photograph: U.Name.Me Derivative work: TeKaBe',
    'CC BY-SA 4.0',
    'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0',
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Purple_Purpur_(retouched).jpg',
    array['How was Tyrian purple extracted from snails?', 'Why did purple come to signify royalty?', 'How did synthetic dyes change fashion?', 'Who were the Phoenicians?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Tyrian purple',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrian_purple',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'bioluminescence',
    'Living light',
    'Bioluminescence is light produced by a chemical reaction inside a living organism, typically when an enzyme called luciferase acts on a molecule called luciferin. It has evolved many separate times. In the open ocean, a large proportion of animals can make light, using it to find mates, lure prey or confuse predators. On land, fireflies flash coded signals and some fungi glow faintly. Glowing waves on beaches usually come from dinoflagellates.',
    'nature',
    array['ocean', 'animals', 'chemistry', 'deep-sea']::text[],
    'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/76/Photinus_pyralis_Firefly_glowing.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled',
    'A firefly glowing in the dark',
    'art farmer from evansville indiana, usa',
    'CC BY-SA 2.0',
    'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0',
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photinus_pyralis_Firefly_glowing.jpg',
    array['Why has bioluminescence evolved so many times?', 'How do fireflies control their flashes?', 'What makes some beaches glow blue at night?', 'How do scientists use luciferase in research?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Bioluminescence',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bioluminescence',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'year-without-a-summer',
    '1816: the year without a summer',
    'In April 1815, Mount Tambora in Indonesia erupted in one of the largest eruptions in recorded history. Its sulphur aerosols spread through the stratosphere, reflecting sunlight and cooling much of the Northern Hemisphere. In 1816, snow fell in New England in June, harvests failed across Europe and food prices soared. Kept indoors by gloomy weather near Lake Geneva, Mary Shelley began the story that became Frankenstein.',
    'history',
    array['volcanoes', 'climate', 'europe', 'literature']::text[],
    'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Mount_Tambora_Volcano%2C_Sumbawa_Island%2C_Indonesia.jpg/1280px-Mount_Tambora_Volcano%2C_Sumbawa_Island%2C_Indonesia.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
    'The caldera of Mount Tambora in Indonesia',
    'This image was taken by the NASA Expedition 20 crew.',
    'Public domain',
    null,
    'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Tambora_Volcano,_Sumbawa_Island,_Indonesia.jpg',
    array['How can a volcano cool the whole planet?', 'What happened to people in Indonesia after the eruption?', 'How did the cold summer inspire Frankenstein?', 'Could a similar eruption happen today?']::text[],
    'Wikipedia: Year Without a Summer',
    'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Year_Without_a_Summer',
    2,
    'published'
  ),
  (
    'crispr',
    'CRISPR: editing genes with a bacterial tool',
    'CRISPR began as part of a bacterial immune system that stores snippets of viral DNA and uses them to recognise invaders. In 2012, Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier showed that the Cas9 enzyme could be programmed to cut chosen DNA sequences, work recognised with the 2020 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. CRISPR tools are now widely used in research, and the first CRISPR-based therapy was approved in 2023, raising medical hopes and ethical questions.',
    'science',
    array['genetics', 'biotechnology', 'medicine', 'microbes']::text[],
    'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Streptococcus_pyogenes_Cas9-DNA-RNA_complex_PDB_4OO8.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled',
    'Illustration of the Cas9 protein bound to DNA',
    'Deposition authors: Nishimasu, H., Ishitani, R., Nureki, O.; Visualization author: Synpath',
    'CC0',
